<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Here Is a Map of the Town. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Town Square</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Here Is a Map of the Town.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write descriptive reviews about content or products. Interactive: Write information for a presentation with peers in a coherent manner.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can describe with peers features of the town square for a class presentation (e.g., "Next to the market is the Town Council."). Can write a short descriptive review of a town square attraction, highlighting personal impressions and details (e.g., "The town square was lively and colorful. The market is next to the tall building in the middle of the town, and I enjoyed trying the local food."). Can help organize information about the town square for a presentation by describing its key features and surroundings (e.g., "The town square has a central plaza with a fountain, and there are shops and restaurants around it.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large, simple map of a town square (showing a fountain, statue, market, library, park, town hall, streets, etc.). Students work in pairs to quickly list (write) as many places as they can identify from the map in 2 minutes. The teacher asks, "What places do you see in the town square?" to prompt initial responses. Students share their lists orally, focusing on pronunciation of words like "street", "statue", "market".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to specific locations on the map and models descriptive sentences using "There is/are" and prepositions of place. For example, "There is a fountain in the middle of the plaza." "There are shops across from the park." The teacher writes these sentences on the board. The teacher also models the pronunciation of words starting with /s/ such as "street," "square," "statue," emphasizing the initial /s/ sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of location descriptions. "Is the market *behind* the library or *next to* it?" (Pointing to the map). "If I say, 'There is a tall monument,' what does 'tall' mean?" "Can we use 'There are' for one building?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Town Square".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short descriptive review of a fictional "Vibrant Town Square" (approximately 5-7 sentences). The text describes the main features of the square, its atmosphere, and provides simple directions for finding a specific attraction within it, using "There is/are", prepositions, adjectives (busy, historic, lively), and a few sequencing words (First, Then). Example: "The town square is very lively and colorful. There is a historic fountain in the center. Next to the fountain, you can find a big market. First, walk straight ahead, then turn left at the traffic light, and the library is on your right."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will interpret written descriptions of locations and use this as a model for their own writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What kind of place is the Vibrant Town Square?" (Students write a one-sentence answer.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read statements about the text and mark them T or F (e.g., "The fountain is small." "The market is next to the fountain."). They correct the false statements in writing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match descriptions of places (e.g., "A place for books") to the correct noun from the text (e.g., "library").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using "There is/there are," prepositions of place (near, between, across from, beside, next to, behind, in front of, on the corner of), imperatives (turn, walk, cross, follow), sequencing words (First, Second, Then, After that, Finally), and target vocabulary (town square, map, statue, monument, plaza, fountain, street, building, market, park, library, town hall, busy, quiet, historic, tall).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Builders (10 min):</b> The teacher provides sentence frames and prompts.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Fill-in-the-blanks:</b> Students complete sentences describing a given map (Projected on the board) using appropriate prepositions and vocabulary. Example: "The (park) is _______ (the library)." "_______ (a fountain) in the (plaza)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Scramble:</b> Students unscramble words to form grammatically correct sentences describing locations on the map. Example: "is / a / next / school / there / the / to / bank." → "There is a school next to the bank."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Imperative Sequencing:</b> Students complete short direction sequences with sequencing words. Example: "______, walk straight. ______, turn left. _______, you will see the market." (First, Then, Finally)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Map Description (8 min):</b> In pairs, students receive a different, simplified map of a town square. Each student writes 3-4 sentences describing a specific landmark or area on their map, making sure to use "There is/are" and prepositions. They then write 2-3 sentences giving directions from a starting point to a destination on the map, using imperatives and sequencing words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check & Feedback (5 min):</b> Students swap their written descriptions and directions with their partner. They check each other's work for grammatical accuracy (There is/are, prepositions, imperatives), correct vocabulary usage, and clarity. They provide written feedback using a simple checklist (e.g., "Did you use 'There is/are' correctly?", "Are the directions easy to follow?"). This reinforces collaborative interaction and helps organize information coherently.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">23 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Town Square".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Writing – "My Favorite Spot in the Town Square Review."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students imagine they have just visited a town square (either the one from the warm-up, a new one, or their local town square).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to write a short descriptive review (minimum 5-7 sentences) about their "favorite" attraction or area within that town square.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They should describe its features, its surroundings, and highlight personal impressions or details. They must include at least two uses of "There is/are," three prepositions of place, and two adjectives (e.g., historic, busy, quiet, spacious).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Additionally, they should include a short piece of information (1-2 sentences) about how to get to another nearby point of interest from their favorite spot, using an imperative and a sequencing word (e.g., "From the park, turn right at the traffic light, and the library is on your left.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The writing will serve as part of a potential class presentation in the next lesson.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency and coherence in written description, applying learned grammar and vocabulary in a creative, personal context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the writing task, collection of written reviews for feedback, and student self-assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My Favorite Spot in the Town Square Review" for teacher feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of "There is/are" and prepositions of place to describe locations (Accuracy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of target vocabulary (e.g., town square, monument, market, park, adjectives like busy, historic, tall) (Range).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the descriptive review and directions (Organization).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of imperatives and sequencing words for directions (Accuracy).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I wrote well today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I want to improve in my writing next time is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of describing the town square in writing was easiest for you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new vocabulary or grammar did you use today that you feel confident with?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., specific practice activities, peer feedback).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face in their writing (e.g., preposition usage, sentence structure, sequencing words)? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson to address these?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did students integrate "There is/are," prepositions, and target vocabulary?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The descriptive reviews written in this lesson will be used and integrated into a class presentation or a creative project in Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), where students will collaboratively share their descriptions and potentially illustrate a map based on their collective writings.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>